  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Where you are from",
     "texto": [
      "**Откуда вы?** = where are you from? (formal; informal: **Откуда ты?**).",
      "You answer with **из** (from) and the name changes to the **genitive** case: Испания → **из Испании**, Россия → **из России**, Москва → **из Москвы**, Мадрид → **из Мадрида**.",
      "As a first guide, many feminine nouns ending in **-а/-я** end in **-ы/-и**; nouns ending in **-ия** end in **-ии**. There are other patterns and exceptions, which you will learn with examples."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Nationality has gender",
     "texto": [
      "A man says **Я испанец** and a woman says **Я испанка**.",
      "For «Russian»: **русский** (man) and **русская** (woman)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The gender of nouns",
     "texto": [
      "You can almost always tell from the last letter. Ends in a **consonant**: masculine (студент, врач, дом). Ends in **-а** or **-я**: feminine (студентка, машина, семья). Ends in **-о** or **-е**: neuter (окно, море).",
      "Grammatical gender does not always match the person's gender. **Врач** and **инженер** have a masculine form, but they can refer to a woman: **Наташа — врач**, **Лаура — инженер**. In English, the word stays the same: «doctor» and «engineer».",
      "The pronouns are **он** (he), **она** (she) and **оно** (it)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
